            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Sun and the Moon</strong><br>\nAmong his various teachings, Anaxagoras had interesting theories about celestial bodies. He thought that the sun and the moon were not gods but rather large objects. He suggested that the sun was a mass of molten metal and that lunar eclipses were caused by the moon passing through the Earth's shadow.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pericles and Anaxagoras</strong><br>\nAnaxagoras formed a close relationship with the powerful Athenian statesman Pericles. He became Pericles' mentor, influencing his views on democracy and science. However, his relationship with Pericles also drew political suspicion and envy from Pericles' enemies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Anaxagoras's Trial</strong><br>\nThe philosophical views of Anaxagoras, especially his thoughts on the sun and moon being mere objects, were controversial. His beliefs contradicted religious views of the time and eventually led to his prosecution for impiety. The trial was a significant event, showing the tension between new ideas and traditional beliefs.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
