          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Death</strong><br>\nAfter his trial, Anaxagoras was imprisoned but later released or exiled, depending on different historical accounts. He spent his final years in Lampsacus, where he continued to write and teach until his death around 428 BCE. Despite the controversies he faced, his work continued to influence philosophers after him.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Future Philosophers</strong><br>\nAnaxagoras left behind a legacy that shaped the world of philosophy. His ideas on nous and the cosmic order were studied and expanded upon by later philosophers like Socrates, Plato, and Aristotle, who all acknowledged his contribution to their own thinking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nToday, Anaxagoras is remembered as a pioneer who opened new ways of understanding the world. His work signaled a shift from mythological explanations of the universe to one based on observation and reason. His efforts carved a path for future scientific inquiry and rational thought.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
